        <div id="black-edition" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Black Edition, Label-Checked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Black Edition is Huel's high-protein meal powder. Huel's formula page, using the Strawberry Banana flavor, lists a 90 g serving with 390 calories, 40 g of protein, 28 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of sugar, 9 g of fiber and 15 g of fat, including some MCTs. The protein comes from pea and brown rice protein, with smaller amounts from flaxseed. Fiber varies by flavor, and Huel's own text quotes a lower range for some.
          </p>

          <p>
            Black Edition is sweetened with stevia and a small amount of organic coconut sugar, per Huel, with no artificial sweeteners. That is a different sweetener system from Complete Protein, which matters if you avoid sucralose. There is also an unflavored and unsweetened version, which is the one to pick if you dislike stevia or want to control sweetness yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a meal, the numbers are strong. Forty grams of protein, a decent dose of fiber, healthy fats and 27 vitamins and minerals in 400 calories will keep most people full for hours, and it is far better than skipping lunch or grabbing a pastry. Huel sells it in 17-meal bags, and the range of flavors is wide, from chocolate and vanilla to cinnamon roll and salted caramel.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a protein shake, it is the wrong tool. If you drink Black Edition after training on top of your normal meals, you add around 400 calories a day, which is roughly 2,800 a week. That can stall a fat-loss diet quickly. Use it to replace a meal, or use Complete Protein when you only want the protein. The two products look similar on the shelf but do completely different jobs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Black Edition Ready-to-drink follows the same logic in a bottle: 35 g of protein from pea and faba bean protein in about 400 calories, per Huel. It is the most convenient option in the range and the most expensive per gram of protein. It makes sense for travel days and busy mornings, not as your everyday protein source.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="meal-vs-shake" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Huel vs a Protein Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            The question behind searches for Huel versus protein powder is usually whether Huel can replace a whey or plant protein tub. The answer depends on which Huel product you mean. Complete Protein can, because it is built as a protein supplement. Huel Powder and Black Edition cannot, because they are meals that happen to contain protein, with the carbohydrate, fat and calories that come with that.
          </p>

          <p>
            A useful rule is calories per gram of protein. A lean protein powder sits around 4.5 to 6 calories per gram of protein. Complete Protein lands at about 5.5. Black Edition sits at about 10, and Huel's original Ready-to-drink at about 20, because it delivers 20 g of protein in a 400-calorie bottle. The higher the number, the more the product behaves like food rather than a supplement.
          </p>

