        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Sweeteners, Testing and Ownership
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sweeteners differ across the range. Huel's US ingredient list for Complete Protein includes sucralose. Black Edition and the Black Edition bottles are labelled as having no artificial sweeteners and use stevia, with a little coconut sugar in the powder. Daily Superblend also states no artificial sweeteners. If you avoid sucralose, Complete Protein is the one Huel product to check carefully before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            We could not find NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice certification for Huel's protein products at the time of writing. Huel publishes detailed formula pages for each product, and the Daily Superblend listing says it is third-party tested without naming a program. That is more transparency than many brands offer, but it is not the same as a sport certificate that screens each batch for banned substances.
          </p>

          <p>
            For drug-tested athletes, that is the deciding factor. If you compete in a tested sport, choose a plant protein with a certificate you can look up by product and flavor, such as Garden of Life Sport, which carries NSF Certified for Sport status per its listing. Our <a href="/garden-of-life-protein-powder-review/">Garden of Life protein powder review</a> covers that range in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            On ownership, Danone, the food group behind brands including Activia and Silk, now owns Huel, after completing the acquisition in September 2026. Reported deal values were close to 1 billion euros, though Danone did not confirm a price. For buyers, the practical effect so far is nil. The thing to watch over the next year is whether formulas, prices or the subscription model change as Huel moves inside a much larger company.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Taste, Texture and Digestion
          </h2>

          <p>
            We have not tasted Huel, so this is buyer feedback. Black Edition gets the most consistent praise for flavor among the Huel powders, with chocolate, vanilla and the dessert flavors popular, and many buyers find it thick and filling. Complete Protein's reviews are more mixed; its Amazon listing sat at about 3.7 out of 5 stars at the time of writing, against about 4.0 for the Black Edition listing we checked.
          </p>

          <p>
            Texture complaints are common with plant proteins and Huel is no exception. Some buyers describe a slightly gritty or earthy finish, especially with less water. Huel's own directions for Complete Protein suggest one level scoop in about 10 fl oz of cold water, shaken for 10 to 20 seconds. Using a blender, adding ice or mixing with plant milk tends to improve mouthfeel significantly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Digestion is the other recurring theme. Black Edition contains a meaningful dose of fiber, and plant proteins can cause gas or bloating in people who are not used to them. Some buyers report this settles after a week or two. Starting with half a serving and building up is a sensible approach, and anyone with a sensitive gut or a diagnosed digestive condition should check with a doctor or dietitian first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because flavors vary so much in reception, start with a single bag, or a variety box if Huel offers one for the product you want, before setting up a subscription. Subscriptions on Huel's site can usually be paused or changed, but it is easier to avoid a stack of bags you dislike than to work through them. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-smoothies/">best protein powder for smoothies</a> guide has ideas for masking plant protein textures.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Huel, and Who Should Skip
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Complete Protein if you want a vegan protein shake with added vitamins and minerals, are happy with sucralose and do not need a sport certification. It suits people topping up protein between meals and plant-based eaters who want something more nutritionally rounded than a single-ingredient pea isolate. Plan on a larger serving after hard training sessions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy Black Edition if you regularly skip breakfast or lunch and want a fast, filling, high-protein meal. It is one of the better options in the meal replacement category on protein per serving and fiber. It also suits people trying to gain weight or eat more consistently. It does not suit people who want a low-calorie protein top-up, because every serving is a full meal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip Huel if you are a drug-tested athlete, if you want the lowest cost per gram of protein, or if you want a dairy-based shake. Whey isolates deliver more leucine per gram and usually cost less, and single-source pea proteins are far cheaper. Skip Black Edition in particular if you are dieting and plan to drink it on top of your normal meals.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are pregnant, breastfeeding, managing kidney disease or taking medication, check with a doctor or registered dietitian before using meal replacements or protein supplements, Huel included. This review is not medical advice. For other plant options worth comparing, see our <a href="/owyn-protein-powder-review/">Owyn protein powder review</a> and the full <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">plant-based protein ranking</a>.
          </p>
