            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much protein is in Huel Black Edition?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Huel's label lists 40 g of plant protein per 90 g serving of Black Edition powder, from pea and brown rice protein with some flaxseed, in about 390 to 400 calories. The bottled Black Edition Ready-to-drink lists 35 g per bottle. Figures can vary slightly by flavor, so check the label of the one you buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Huel Complete Protein good for building muscle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be, as part of enough total daily protein. A 20 g scoop of plant protein carries less leucine than the same amount of whey, so after training a scoop and a half, about 30 g of protein, is a sensible target. Total protein across the day and consistent training matter far more than which brand you choose.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Huel contain sucralose?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some Huel products do. Huel's US ingredient list for Complete Protein includes sucralose. Black Edition powder and Black Edition Ready-to-drink are labelled as having no artificial sweeteners and use stevia, with a little coconut sugar in the powder. There is also an unflavored, unsweetened Black Edition. Check the specific product and flavor before buying.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Huel third-party tested?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We could not find NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice certification for Huel's protein products at the time of writing. Huel publishes detailed formula pages, and the Daily Superblend listing says it is third-party tested without naming a program. Drug-tested athletes should choose a certified product whose status they can look up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who owns Huel now?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Danone completed its acquisition of Huel in September 2026, after agreeing the deal in March and receiving UK competition clearance in August, per Danone and press coverage. Huel was founded in the UK in 2015 by Julian Hearn and James Collier. Huel has said its leadership team will stay in place, and the labels have not changed because of the deal so far.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Huel vs protein powder: which should I buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buy a protein powder, including Huel Complete Protein, if you already eat regular meals and want an extra 20 to 30 g of protein for few calories. Buy a Huel meal, such as Black Edition, if you often skip meals and want a fast, filling replacement. The deciding question is whether you are adding protein or replacing a meal.
              </p>
            </div>

